# PostgreSQL called the printf family without consulting the return, and
# every known standard library implementation of those functions can fail
# with ENOMEM, so a failed call silently omitted output, exposed trailing
# buffer contents, or crashed -- unchecked function returns to information
# exposure (CWE-252/CWE-200, CVE-2015-3166; the May 2015 security release).
# The fixing commit 16304a013432931e61e623c8d85e9fe24709d9ba, "Add
# error-throwing wrappers for the printf family of functions", remediates
# the callers architecturally rather than at any call site:
# src/include/port.h #defines printf/fprintf/sprintf/vsnprintf/... to
# *_throw_on_fail wrappers in the new src/port/syswrap.c, each of which
# checks `ret < 0` and aborts (elog(ERROR) in the backend, abort() in the
# frontend) instead of returning, and the one file that bypasses the
# wrappers -- src/port/snprintf.c, the replacement implementation built
# under USE_REPL_SNPRINTF -- gains explicit `< 0` tests of its own.
#
# ## Re-measured at the current tip
#
# Three `--cache off` runs per revision (timeout 180, --max-ram 6GB) over
# src/port/snprintf.c: 34 findings at the parent cac18a76b and 32 at the
# fix, stable in all six runs -- 4 VYQL-MEM-002, 29 vs 27 VYQL-MEM-006 and
# 1 VYQL-MEM-016. The two-finding delta is NullableDeref heuristics on the
# guards the fix *adds*, so the safe revision scores higher; nothing fires
# on the unchecked returns. The `-flags only` view reports 129 vs 125
# flags with the same shape. Scanning the whole src/port directory reports
# 25 findings at or above high at both revisions, 3/3 runs each.
# `vyql match` labels the four sprintf calls in fmtptr/fmtfloat only
# `code.UnboundedCopySmell` -- a judgement about the destination buffer
# with no relation to the return -- at both revisions.
#
# ## Why the miss holds
#
# The remediation is invisible at every call site: src/backend/main/main.c
# carries thirty discarded printf calls and is byte-identical at the
# parent and the fix, so caller-side detection cannot discriminate this
# pair in principle. The direct CWE-252 form is measured unshippable as a
# rule: 4610 statement-discarded printf-family call sites stand in this
# tree against 37 that capture the return (882 discarded in src/backend
# alone), and the upstream author's own message calls editing each site
# "painfully invasive". The format-length family that has shipped since
# the first pass -- VYQL-MEM-073, code.MissingFormatTruncationCheck over
# analysis.format_length.truncation_unchecked -- is scoped to the bounded
# spellings by its own contract ("the unbounded sprintf spellings have no
# bound to consult and are not reported here at all"), and its guard is
# bound consultation, which sprintf has nothing to consult. The one
# dataflow-visible slice, fmtfloat's `convert[vallen - 1]` reading past
# the buffer when the captured sprintf return is negative, is a CWE-787
# slice of this fix rather than the recorded weakness, and covering it
# needs a second per-shape sibling in that observation family with
# different guard semantics -- a new fact, concept and rule, not a
# mechanical correction. Its measured precision is good (87 raw sites
# across 78,076 C files in the corpus, 2 here at the parent and 0 at the
# fix), so it is worth its own rank rather than this one. No definition
# change shipped. All forms pin the miss as a double reject of the
# family's consumer: when checked-return vocabulary reaches the standard
# formatted-output functions, the discarded form and the
# consumed-as-length form must flip to expect VYQL-VAL-001, and the
# unbounded spelling of the length family to expect VYQL-MEM-073.

